.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt CRONTAB 5
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm crontab
.Nd tables of scheduled commands
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A
.Nm
file lists commands for
.Xr cron 8
to run, and when.
Users' tables are installed with
.Xr crontab 1 ;
the system's are
.Pa /etc/crontab
and the files in
.Pa /etc/cron.d .
.Pp
Each line is one of:
.Bl -bullet
.It
a blank line, or a comment, beginning with
.Ql # ;
.It
an environment setting,
.Ar name Li = Ar value ,
with or without blanks around the
.Ql = .
The value may be quoted with
.Ql \(aq
or
.Ql \(dq ,
which is the way to give it leading or trailing blanks.
Settings apply to every job of the table;
.Ql MAILTO=\(dq\(dq
discards the jobs' output;
.It
a job: five time fields, then, in a system table only, the user to run it as,
then the command, which is the rest of the line.
.El
.Ss Time fields
The fields are, in order:
.Bl -column "day of the month" "0-7, or sun-sat"
.It Sy field Ta Sy values
.It minute Ta 0-59
.It hour Ta 0-23
.It day of the month Ta 1-31
.It month Ta 1-12, or jan-dec
.It day of the week Ta 0-7, or sun-sat
.El
.Pp
Sunday is both 0 and 7.
Names are three letters, in any case, and may be used anywhere a number may.
A field is
.Ql *
(every value), a number, a range
.Ql 1-5 ,
or a list
.Ql 1,15,30
of numbers and ranges; any of these may have a step,
.Ql /n ,
which takes every
.Ar n Ns th
value: so
.Ql */15
in the minute is 0, 15, 30 and 45, and
.Ql 5/20
is 5, 25 and 45.
.Pp
A job runs in a minute that matches all of the minute, hour and month.
If both day fields are restricted, a day that matches either one counts; if
either begins with
.Ql * ,
a day must match both.
So
.Ql 0 0 13 * 5
runs on the 13th of each month and on every Friday, while
.Ql 0 0 */2 * 5
runs on Fridays that fall on an odd day of the month.
.Ss Special times
The five fields may be replaced by one of:
.Bl -column "@every_second" "0 0 1 1 *"
.It Cm @reboot Ta once, when Xr cron 8 starts after the system boots
.It Cm @yearly Ta Li "0 0 1 1 *" ; also Cm @annually
.It Cm @monthly Ta Li "0 0 1 * *"
.It Cm @weekly Ta Li "0 0 * * 0"
.It Cm @daily Ta Li "0 0 * * *" ; also Cm @midnight
.It Cm @hourly Ta Li "0 * * * *"
.It Cm @every_minute Ta Li "* * * * *"
.It Cm @every_second Ta every second
.El
.Ss The command
The command is run with
.Ql $SHELL -c .
An unescaped
.Ql %
ends it: the text after it is the command's standard input, each further
.Ql %
standing for a new line.
.Ql \e%
is a
.Ql %
in either part.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A user's table:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
MAILTO=""
# Every 15 minutes during working hours, Monday to Friday.
*/15	9-17	*	*	mon-fri	$HOME/bin/sync-mail
# On the first of the month, two lines added to a file.
0	9	1	*	*	cat >> $HOME/todo%Pay the rent.%Back up the laptop.
@reboot	$HOME/bin/start-agent
.Ed
.Pp
A system table line, run as
.Dq operator :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
30	2	*	*	*	operator	/usr/local/bin/backup
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crontab 1 ,
.Xr cron 8 ,
.Xr periodic 8
.Sh HISTORY
OxideBSD's format is Paul Vixie's, as on the BSDs, with
.Fx Ns 's
.Cm @every_minute
and
.Cm @every_second .
